== Seeking Marriage

=== §. 1. #emph[Of those who may seek to be married.]

#smallcaps[Having] laid down in the former treatise the foundation of all domestic duties, by expounding the words of the apostle, I now intend to set them out distinctly in order, beginning with the first and chief couple in the family, #emph[man] and #emph[wife.]

Here we must first speak of the #emph[persons] who are to be counted true and lawful #emph[husband] and #emph[wife,] and then of the #emph[duties] which they owe each other.

So this treatise consists of two parts.

+ The first declares who are man and wife.
+ The second sets out those common mutual duties which they owe one another. First, of the first.

Husband and wife are those who are rightly joined together #footnote[Who are husband and wife.] by the bond of marriage, #footnote[See #emph[Treatise] 1, §. 82. #emph[&] 84.] by which two are made “one flesh.”

To clear this point better, we will consider both the #emph[parties] that may be so closely joined together, and also the #emph[manner] in which they are rightly joined in so firm a unity.

About the #emph[parties] we must inquire:

+ Who may seek a mate for marriage.
+ What kind of mate is to be taken.

1\. All those who are able, without inevitable danger to their #footnote[Who may marry.] yokefellow, to perform the essential duties of marriage may be married.

From this proposition arise three questions to be discussed.

+ Who are to be counted #emph[able?]
+ What danger is inevitable?
+ Whether marriage is free for all but these?

=== §. 2. #emph[Of ripeness of years in those who are to be married.]

1\. Those are to be counted able who have #footnote[#emph[Ephebi. Vt iustas nuptias contrahant, sint masculi puberes, foeminae viripotentes.] \[Youths. To contract a lawful marriage, the males must have reached puberty and the females be of marriageable age.\] #emph[Iustin. Instit. l.] 1. #emph[tit.] 10.] passed the flower of their age, and are not made impotent by a defect of nature or by any other cause.

1\. Ripeness of years is absolutely necessary for consummating a just and lawful marriage. Therefore, as God at first made Adam of full age, so when he sought out a wife for him, he made her of full age too: he made her a #emph[woman,] not a child. #footnote[Gen 2:22. 1 Cor 7:36.] Where the apostle advises parents to take care for the marriage of their children, he puts in this proviso: “if they are past the flower of their age.” Childhood is counted the flower of age. While the flower of the plant is sprouting, the seed is green, unfit to be sown.

#emph[Question.] How long does the flower of age last?

#emph[Answer.] #footnote[#emph[Justin. Instit. l.] 1. #emph[tit.] 22. Second part of #emph[Reports of Cases.] 35. #emph[Hen.] 6. #emph[fol.] 41. #emph[b.]] The civil law, and the common law also, set down twelve years for the flower of a female’s age, and fourteen for a male’s, which is the least. For before those years they can have no need of marriage, nor are they yet well fit for marriage. So if they hold off some years longer, it will be much better for the parties themselves who marry, #footnote[#emph[Est iuvenum coniugium incommodum liberorum procreationi. Cum enim in omnibus animantibus imperfecti sunt iuniorum partus, ac multo etiam magis foeminarum, id etiam fiat in hominibus necesse est.] \[The marriage of the young is bad for the bearing of children. For since in all animals the offspring of the young are imperfect, and those of the females much more so, the same must happen in human beings also.\] #emph[Arist. Pol. lib.] 7.] for the children whom they bring forth, for the family of which they are the head, and for the commonwealth of which they are members. Note the ages of the kings of Israel and Judah when they were first married, and we will find few of them to have been under twenty, and those few not more than one or two years under. And yet of all sorts of people the kings used to marry the soonest, so that they might have heirs early.

#emph[Objection.] Solomon#footnote[1 Chr 22:5; 29:1.] was only a child when he came to the crown, and yet #footnote[2 Chr 12:13.] he then had a child at least a year old.

#emph[Answer.] He was said to be a child not absolutely but comparatively: #footnote[1 Kgs 2:22.] in relation to his other brothers, who were older than he, and #footnote[1 Kgs 3:7.] with respect to the great work he was to undertake. In the time of his reign he is said to be #emph[old,]#footnote[1 Kgs 11:4.] which could not be if he had been a child in years when he began to reign, for he reigned only forty years.

2\. #emph[Objection.] Ahaz was only twenty years old when he began to reign, and reigned only sixteen years, and yet when he died Hezekiah his son was twenty-five years old, #footnote[2 Chr 28:1; 29:1.] by which reckoning Ahaz had a child when he was only eleven, or twelve years old at most.

1\. #emph[Answer.] Some say that this was extraordinary, and give this reason: “Ahaz, as young a father as Elizabeth was an old mother,” #footnote[#emph[Broughton] in his Concent of Script.] “should have hoped in Emmanuel, born of a virgin.”

2\. #emph[Answer.] The beginning of that reign, when Ahaz was only twenty years old, is to be referred to Jotham his father. For Ahaz was twenty years old when #emph[he] (namely Jotham) began to reign, just as Jehoiachin#footnote[2 Chr 36:9.] was eight years old when #emph[he] (namely his father Jehoiakim) began to reign: for Jehoiachin was #footnote[2 Kgs 24:8.] eighteen when he himself began to reign.

#emph[Objection.] Jotham was only #footnote[2 Chr 27:1.] twenty-five years old when he began to reign. How then could Ahaz his son be twenty at that time?

#emph[Answer.] Jotham was twenty-five years old when his father Uzziah was struck with leprosy,#footnote[2 Chr 26:21.] and from that time he reigned as king, even in his father’s lifetime. But after his father was dead, the kingdom was established on him alone #footnote[Parents sin in marrying children under age.] in a more solemn manner, and in this respect it is said that he then began to reign. And by then his son Ahaz was twenty years old, Jotham himself being about forty.

Contrary to this fitness of age is the practice of #footnote[#emph[Puella dum ei aetas matura non est, nulli dari vel promitti debet.] \[A girl, while she is not yet of ripe age, ought not to be given or promised to anyone.\] #emph[Aug. epist.] 233. #emph[Matrimonium contractum verum non est si impubes fuerit qui contraxit.] \[A marriage contracted is not a true marriage if the one who contracted it was under age.\] #emph[Azpilcuet. Consil. & resp. l.] 4. #emph[de spons. Cons.] 8.] those parents, or other relatives of children, who make matches for them in their childhood, move them to consent, and so cause them to be married. Such marriages are mock marriages and mere nullities. For children cannot know what belongs to marriage, much less can they perform what is required of married persons. Their consent therefore is justly counted no consent, unless they ratify it after they come of age.

=== §. 3. #emph[Of impotent persons who ought not to seek after marriage.]

2\. Those are to be counted #emph[impotent,] and in that respect unable to perform the essential duties of marriage, who (to use the Scripture phrase) “were born eunuchs from their mother’s #footnote[Matt 19:12.] womb,” or are made so by some accident: such as those who are defective or closed in their private parts, or struck with an incurable paralysis, or afflicted with frigidity, or any other such impediment.

These ought not to seek after marriage, for by those signs of impotence God shows that he calls them to live single.

Those sin against this manifestation of God’s will #footnote[A sin to conceal any impotence.] who conceal their impotence and join themselves in marriage. By this they frustrate one main end of marriage, which is the procreation of children, and do such wrong to the party whom they marry that sufficient amends can never be made.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of barrenness, that it does not hinder marriage.]

#emph[Question.] Are those who are barren to be ranked among those impotent persons?

#emph[Answer.] No, there is a great difference between impotence and barrenness.

1\. Impotence may be known #footnote[#emph[Sterilem coniugem fas non est relinquere.] \[It is not lawful to leave a barren spouse.\] #emph[Aug. de Nup. lib.] 1. #emph[cap.] 10.] and discerned by outward, perceptible signs; barrenness cannot: it is not discerned except by the lack of childbearing.

2\. Impotent persons cannot render due benevolence (the marriage duty each owes the other), but those who are barren can.

3\. Impotence is incurable, but barrenness is not absolutely so. Many, after they have been barren a long while, have become fruitful: and that not only by an extraordinary work of God above the course of nature (as Sarah#footnote[Gen 18:11.] and Elizabeth,#footnote[Luke 1:7.] who because of age were past the time of childbearing), but also by such a blessing as might stand with the course of nature, obtained by prayer (as Rebekah#footnote[Gen 25:21.] and Hannah),#footnote[1 Sam 1:5, 20.] of which daily experience gives good evidence. For many have brought forth children after 10, 15, 20 and more years of barrenness.

On these grounds many saints who have been barren have married, and their practice in this was not disallowed, nor their marriage dissolved. #footnote[#emph[Tantum valet sociale vinculum coniugum, vt cùm causa procreandi colligitur, nec ipsa causa procreandi soluetur. Aug. de bon. Coniug. cap.] 7.] For though the procreation of children is one end of marriage, yet it is not the only end. And the marriage bond is so inviolable that, though it is made for the sake of children, yet it must not be broken for lack of children.

=== §. 5. #emph[Of that inevitable danger which hinders marriage.]

3\. Those who are infected with such contagious diseases as spread to those who keep company with them, and infect them also, ought not to seek after marriage, for that cannot but turn to the danger of the party whom they marry. It was for the mutual good of one another #footnote[Gen 2:18.] that God ordained the law of marriage. To use it to the hurt and danger of one another is against the main end of its first institution.

The law of shutting a leper off from all society with men#footnote[2 Chr 26:21.] proves as much. For if lepers were not allowed to have mutual society with any man, much less were they allowed to have the society of marriage with a wife or a husband.

By contagious diseases not only will both the parties who keep company together be infected, but also their offspring. In this way their disease, which otherwise might die with themselves, is passed on to their posterity. A like restraint may be applied to such foul and loathsome diseases as make the company and society of the infected person irksome and odious to their companion.

Those sin against the end and use of marriage #footnote[A sin to conceal a contagious disease.] who conceal such diseases and so join themselves in marriage, to the irreparable harm of the party whom they marry.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of the lawfulness of marriage to all sorts of persons.]

Where there is no such just impediment as has been mentioned before, it is lawful for all sorts of people, of whatever #footnote[ἐν πᾶσι.] calling or condition, to marry. For “marriage is honorable #footnote[Heb 13:4.] in all,” or among all, namely in or among all sorts of people. That is why it is counted “a doctrine of demons” to “prohibit #footnote[1 Tim 4:1, 3.] marriage.” For it is a doctrine contrary to God’s word, and a doctrine that causes much inward burning and outward pollution, and so makes their bodies, which should be temples of the Holy Spirit, sties of devils.

The disease for whose remedy marriage is sanctified is a common disease, which has infected all sorts of people. Why then should the remedy not be as common?

In this case the apostle speaks generally of all, without exception of any: “to avoid fornication, each man should have his own #footnote[1 Cor 7:2, 9.] wife, and each woman her own husband.” And again: “If they cannot control themselves, let them marry. For it is better to marry than to burn with passion.”

#emph[Objection.] “There are eunuchs who have made themselves eunuchs”#footnote[Matt 19:12.] (that is, have abstained from marriage and lived a single #footnote[A devilish doctrine to forbid marriage.] life with self-control) “for the sake of the kingdom of heaven.”

#emph[Answer.] That is spoken of some particular persons to whom the gift of self-control was given, not of any distinct conditions and callings, as if each and every one of this or that calling had done so, or were able to do so. That is why Christ adds this clause, “The one who can accept this should accept it,” and the apostle says to the same purpose, “each one has his own gift from God.”#footnote[1 Cor 7:7.]

Contrary to this necessary and lawful liberty is the impure and tyrannical restraint of the Church of Rome, by which all who enter any of their holy orders are kept from marriage.

Do they not in this “test God” by placing on men’s necks “a yoke that neither we nor our fathers have been able to bear”?#footnote[Acts 15:10.] #footnote[#emph[Vide Chrysost. hom.] 4. #emph[in Isay] 6.] No such restraint was ever laid by God’s word on any of those holy functions which he ordained. For under the law it was lawful for high priests, ordinary priests, all sorts of Levites and extraordinary prophets to marry; and under the gospel, for #footnote[#emph[Omnes Apostoli exceptis Iohanne & Paulo vxores habuerunt.] \[All the apostles except John and Paul had wives.\] #emph[Ambr. in] 2 Cor 11.] apostles, bishops, deacons and all ministers of the word. #footnote[#emph[Tolle de Ecclesia honorabile connubium, nonne reples eam concubinarijs incestuosis, seminifluis mollibus, masculorum concubitoribus, & omni denique genere immundorum?] \[Take honorable marriage out of the church, and do you not fill it with incestuous keepers of concubines, with the effeminate who pollute themselves, with men who lie with males, and in short with every kind of unclean person?\] #emph[Bernard. in Cant. Serm.] 66. #emph[vide Bernard. de Conuers. ad Cler. cap.] 29.] Fearful have been the effects of this diabolical doctrine: fornication, adultery, incest, sodomy, bestiality, and who knows what else. Many wives have been put away from their husbands because their husbands were ministers, and many ministers put out of their calling because they had wives. Many children have by this means been born out of wedlock, and many of them cruelly murdered in their infancy. #footnote[Huldricus, seu vt alij, Volusianus in Epist. ad Nichol. Pap.] Six thousand heads of infants were found in the ponds of one religious house. How many more thousands have from time to time been cast into other ponds, or buried in gardens or other places, or in other ways conveyed out of sight? That doctrine must be devilish which has such devilish effects. #footnote[#emph[Vtinam qui continere non valent perfectionem temerariè profiteri, aut coelebatui dare nomina vererentur. Sumptuosa siquidem turris est, & verbum grande, quod non omnes capere possunt.] \[For it is a costly tower, and a great word, which not all can receive.\] #emph[Bernard. de Conuers. ad Cler. serm.] 29.] Well did he wish who wished that all who cannot control themselves would take heed how they rashly profess perfection and vow virginity.

=== §. 7. #emph[Of the things which are absolutely necessary to make a person fit for marriage.]

Those who have power to marry must be careful in choosing “a suitable helper” for themselves, for #footnote[Gen 2:18. Necessary for the essence of marriage.] this was God’s care when he first instituted marriage. To make a suitable helper for marriage, some things are absolutely necessary for the very essence or being of marriage; others are necessary for the comfort and happiness of marriage.

As to the former sort, there must be chosen:

1\. #emph[One of the same kind] or nature. For among all the creatures that were made, “for Adam no suitable helper was found.”#footnote[Gen 2:20. Lying with beasts.] Therefore God made a woman out of his bone and flesh, of his #footnote[1\. Persons of the same kind.] own nature and kind.

Contrary to this is the detestable sin of lying with beasts, expressly forbidden by the #footnote[Lev 18:23.] law: a sin worse than beastly, for the brute beasts content themselves with their own kind. It is monstrous in its kind, and a cause of abominable monsters. #footnote[#emph[Vide Aug. de Civit. Dei lib.] 15. #emph[cap.] 23. #emph[& Lud. Vivem in eundem de Incubis, & Succubis.]] Contrary also is that copulation which witches have with devils, than which nothing is more unnatural, nothing more monstrous and odious.

2\. #emph[One of the opposite sex:] the male must choose a female, the female a male. So God, having made Adam a male, #footnote[2\. Persons of different sexes. #emph[Maritalis copula requirit sexuum aptitudinem.] \[The marriage union requires the fitness of the sexes.\] #emph[Bern. in Cant. serm.] 66. Unnatural unions. Rom 1:25, 26.] made Eve a female, and joined them in marriage. Only a union of these different sexes is fit for the increase of mankind and the other marriage duties.

Contrary are those unnatural unions of persons of the same sex, which the apostle lists as judgments inflicted on the heathen, because “they exchanged the truth of God for a lie, and worshiped and served the creature rather than the Creator.”

3\. #emph[One beyond those degrees of kinship by blood and by marriage which] #footnote[Persons outside the forbidden degrees of kinship.] #emph[are forbidden by the law of God.] These degrees are set out by Moses, Lev 18:6, 7, etc., and explained in a table of the degrees of kinship by blood and by marriage within which no one may marry, appointed to be hung up in every church.

Contrary is incest, a sin not only forbidden by God’s #footnote[Incest. #emph[O mulieris scelus incredibile, & praeter hanc vnam in omni vita inauditum. O libidinem effrenatam & indomitam! O audaciam, singularem! Non timuisse vim deorum, hominumque famam? Vicit pudorem libido, timorem audacia, rationem amentia. Cic. pro. A. Cluent.]] word, but so horrible even to the heathen that (to use the apostle’s words, 1 Cor 5:1) it is “not even named among the pagans.” That verdict is excellently confirmed by the heathen orator’s impassioned exclamation against one Sassia, who married her son-in-law, in these words: “O incredible wickedness of a woman, unheard of in any age but this! O unbridled and untamed lust! O singular boldness! Not to fear the power of God and the report of men!” etc. “Lust, shamelessness and madness overcame shame, fear and reason.”

What may we now think of the dispensations which the pope gives for incestuous marriages, allowed to great princes even by the #footnote[§. 24. #emph[cap.] 5.] Council of Trent? Does he not show himself in this to be “the man of lawlessness,” who “opposes and exalts himself above all that is called God”?

4\. #emph[One who is free,]#footnote[2 Thess 2:3, 4. 4. Persons who are free.] neither married nor betrothed to another. The law of marriage shows this much in this clause: “the two will become one flesh.”#footnote[Matt 19:5.] And since #footnote[Deut 22:22, 23, 24.] the law inflicts the same punishment on a betrothed person who commits sexual impurity as it does on a married person, it is evident that it is unlawful to marry one betrothed to another, just as it is to marry one married to another. So binding is a betrothal contract that the law calls a betrothed maiden “a wife,” and a betrothed maiden could not be put away without a certificate of divorce.

Contrary are bigamy and polygamy, spoken of #footnote[#emph[Treatise] 1, §. 83.] before. To this head may be referred marriages with those who have been betrothed to others before. These are utterly unlawful.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of the lawfulness of other marriages after one of the married couple is dead.]

1\. #emph[Question.] Are those who have buried their husband or wife so free that they may marry again?

#emph[Answer.] Yes, as free as those who were never married before. The law not only permits a widow to marry again,#footnote[Deut 25:5, 9.] but if her husband died before he had any children, it commanded the nearest kinsman who was living and free to marry her, so that he might raise up offspring for his dead brother. If he refused to do so, a penalty of disgrace was inflicted on him: the rejected widow was to “remove his sandal” from his foot #footnote[#emph[Non concederet hoc Paulus nisi liceret. At parum dico concedit: vult quoque. Volo, inquit, adolescentiores nubere: nec dubium quin viduas dicat.] \[Paul would not grant this unless it were lawful. But “grants” is too little to say: he also wants it. “I want,” he says, “the younger women to marry”; and no doubt he means widows.\] #emph[Bern. serm.] 66. #emph[in Cant.]] and “spit in his face” in the presence of the elders. The apostle expressly says that a woman, when her husband is dead, is free to be married (1 Cor 7:39). Yes, speaking of young widows, he says further, “I want them to marry” (1 Tim 5:14). This liberty, which the prophet of God and the apostle of Christ grant to a wife, can by no show of reason be denied to a husband. For the bond of marriage gives them a like power over each other’s body (1 Cor 7:4), and binds the one as inviolably as the other (Matt 19:6). Husbands therefore, as well as wives, have used this liberty, as Abraham did (Gen 25:1). #footnote[Rom 7:2.] The apostle who gives this liberty gives a reason for it, taken from the limit of the time in which married persons have power over one another, and that is the time of this life only: “a married woman is bound by law to her husband as long as he lives. But if her husband dies, she is released,” etc. On this ground all the #footnote[§. 24. #emph[Quid largam benedictionem nuptiarum restringis?] \[Why do you restrict the generous blessing of marriage?\] #emph[Bern. loc. citat. Dominus tot maritorum mulierem nulla suae sententiae significatione damnauit.] \[The Lord did not condemn the woman who had had so many husbands by any sign of his own verdict.\] #emph[Aug. de bon. Vid. c.] 12.] reasons which warrant or move those who were never married to marry may be applied to those whose yokefellow has been taken from them by death.

2\. #emph[Question.] May this liberty be extended any further than to a second marriage?

#emph[Answer.] We find no restraint from a third or fourth or more marriages, if by divine providence so many wives or husbands are taken away one after another while there is need for the surviving party to use the benefit of marriage. The woman of Samaria who had five husbands one after another is not blamed for having been married to so many, but for living with one (after the others were dead) who was not her husband (John 4:18). Nor did the Lord condemn the woman who was said to have had seven husbands one after another (Matt 22:25, etc.).

Contrary is the opinion of the #footnote[#emph[Secundas nuptias pro fornicationibus habent. Aug. de haer.] §. 26.] Montanists and Cataphrygians, ancient heretics who counted the marriages which the survivor made after the death of a yokefellow to be adulterous. Tertullian,#footnote[#emph[Tertullianus factus est haereticus quia transiens ad Cataphryges cepit secundas nuptias tanquam stupra damnare.] \[Tertullian became a heretic because, going over to the Cataphrygians, he began to condemn second marriages as whoredom.\] #emph[Ibid.] §. 86.] an ancient and learned church father, was so far infected with this heresy that he wrote a treatise in defense of it. It seems from their arguments that one main ground of their error was a misinterpretation of those Scriptures which forbid men to have two wives at once, and women to have two husbands at once, such as: “The two will become one flesh,” Gen 2:24; a bishop and a deacon must be “the husband of but one wife,” 1 Tim 3:2, 12; “A widow should be enrolled” who has been “the wife of one man,” 1 Tim 5:9. These are indeed express texts against such bigamists and polygamists as have been described #footnote[#emph[Treatise] 1, §. 83.] before; but they make no more against second or later marriages, one mate being dead, than against first marriages. Into the roll of these heretics may be put those who deny such later marriages to any kind of ministers. So do our adversaries: they #footnote[#emph[Ministerijs minorum ordinum exercendis suffici possunt coniugati, dummodo non bigami. Concil. Trid.] §. 23. #emph[c.] 17. #emph[Vide Bellarm. de Cler. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 23.] exclude those who have married again after one wife is dead from such functions of the inferior orders as they admit those who have married only once to. They allege many of the same Scriptures as the Montanists do (as 1 Tim 3:2, 12 and 5:9), which shows that they are infected with the same heresy, though they claim to renounce it.

=== §. 9. #emph[Of equality in years between husband and wife.]

That the fellowship of marriage may prove a comfort, it is needful #footnote[#emph[Aequalem ducito Pittaci dictum. Si vis nubere, nube pari.] \[Marry an equal: the saying of Pittacus. If you would marry, marry an equal.\] #emph[Ouid.] 1. Equality in age.] that there should be some equality between the parties who are married in #emph[Age, Wealth, Rank, Piety.]

1\. For #emph[Age:] as the party who seeks a mate must be of ripe years, fit to give consent and able to perform marriage duties, so the mate who is taken must correspond somewhat #footnote[ἧλιξ ἥλικα. #emph[Arist. Eth. l.] 8. #emph[c.] 12. Luke 1:7.] in age: if one is young, both young; if one is of middle age, both so; if one is advanced in years, the other also. It is noted of Zechariah and Elizabeth that “they were both well along in years.” If both were old together, then both were also young together. Equality in years makes married persons more fit for the procreation of children, for a mutual performance of marriage duties to each other, and for making their company and society in every way happier.

This equality is not to be taken too strictly, as if the married couple had to be of exactly the same age, but only as some correspondence in years. There may be this even though there is a disparity #footnote[Most fitting that husbands be older than wives.] of five or ten or somewhat more years, especially if the excess of years is on the husband’s part. For besides the fact that, in the ordinary course of nature, a man’s strength and vigor last longer than a woman’s, it is very fitting that the husband should be somewhat older than his wife, because he is a head, a governor, a protector of his wife. The Scripture notes many husbands to be older than their wives (as Abraham was ten years older than Sarah;#footnote[Gen 17:17.] and if we closely mark the circumstances of the histories of Isaac and Jacob and their wives, we will find that the husbands were older than their wives). As far as I remember, no approved example of a husband younger than his wife can be given from Scripture.

Contrary to this equality in years is the practice of many #footnote[Unfitting for aged persons to marry young ones.] men and women who, being aged, to satisfy their lust or for some other ulterior motive, marry those who are only in the flower of their age. In this they often fall far short of their expectation. For those young ones, finding the company of aged folk burdensome and irksome to them, soon begin to loathe it, and so cause more grief and vexation than they ever gave comfort and contentment.

On the other side, there are others who in the prime and #footnote[Unfitting for young persons to marry the aged.] strength of their age, for wealth, honor or similar motives, marry those who with age are beginning to be decrepit and unfit to be married, hoping that they will not live long, but that with a little trouble they will gain high rank or riches, and after a while be free again. But God often repays such people in kind, by prolonging the life of those aged persons, and so making the burden much more grievous and tedious than was imagined, and by taking away those young ones sooner than they expected, so that all their hopes perish. #footnote[#emph[Ne discrepent aetates sic vt vir suscipere liberos possit; mulier non possit; aut haec possit, ille non possit. Ex his enim seditiones, discordiaeque, etc.] \[Their ages should not differ so that the man can beget children and the woman cannot, or she can and he cannot. For from these come quarrels and discords, etc.\] #emph[Arist. Pol. lib.] 7.] The heathen observed inequality in years to be the occasion of many harms, and so prescribed rules against it.

=== §. 10. #emph[Of equality in wealth and rank between those who are to be married together.]

2\. Some equality in outward property and wealth is also fitting #footnote[Equality in wealth. #emph[Premitur magno coniuge nupta minor.] \[A bride of lower rank is weighed down by a great husband.\] #emph[Ouid. epist.] 9.] for the parties who are to be married together, so that the disparity in it (especially if it is too great) does not make the one lord it over the other more than is fitting. For if a man of great wealth is married to a poor woman, he will think to make her like his maidservant, and expect her to behave toward him in a way that does not become a yokefellow and a bedfellow, so that such a woman may rather be said to be brought into bondage than into marriage. And if a rich woman marries a poor man, she will expect to be the master and to rule him, so that the order which God has established will be utterly overturned, and the honor of marriage laid in the dust. For where there is no order, there can be no honor.

3\. The same may be said of outward rank, that #footnote[3\. Equality in rank. #emph[Eas vxores quas inferioris gradus homines ducant, hi qui altioris dignitatis sunt ducere legibus propter dignitatem prohibentur.] \[Those of higher rank are forbidden by the laws, for the sake of their rank, to marry such wives as men of lower degree marry.\]] in it also there should be some equality: that princes, nobles and gentlemen marry those of their own rank, and the lower sort those of their own degree. Note what sort of wives Abraham, Isaac and Jacob married, and it will appear that they had regard to this equality. Disparity in rank, as well as in wealth, is a means to make men and women swell and lord it over others more than is fitting, yes, and to cast their former wealth and rank in each other’s teeth.

Contrary on the one side are the practices of those who aim #footnote[#emph[Digest. lib.] 23. #emph[cap.] 2. §. 49. A fault to aim to marry a person much above one’s rank. #emph[Qui longè praestantiores se ducunt, non coniugum viri, sed serui fiunt dotium.] \[Those who marry women far above themselves become not the husbands of wives but the slaves of dowries.\] #emph[Plut. de Instit. liber.] Prov 14:1; 12:4.] to marry above their own wealth and rank, thinking to advance themselves by such marriages. This is the only thing which many seek in seeking wives and husbands, and so it often happens that they meet with the worst matches, and make their marriage a kind of bondage to them. Great portions make many women proud, fussy, lavish, idle and careless. A man would be much better off, even for the sake of his outward wealth, to marry a prudent, sober, thrifty, careful, diligent wife, though with a small portion, than such a one. A proud back, a dainty tooth and a lavish hand will soon consume a great portion; but “every wise woman builds her house,” and “a wife of noble character is her husband’s crown.” Many wives also who are married to very rich husbands are kept shorter and more pinched in their allowance than those who are married to men of lower means. It is not the means a man has, but his mind and disposition, that makes him free and generous to his wife.

Contrary are the marriages which men of great authority #footnote[A fault to marry a person much below one’s rank.] and means make with lowly women, yes, many times with their own maids, and those of the lowest rank, their kitchen maids; and those which women of noble blood and great wealth #footnote[#emph[Semper in coniunctionibus non solum quid liceat considerandum est, sed & quid honestum sit. Digest. lib.] 23. #emph[tit.] 2. §. 42.] make with their servingmen. Do they not in this reveal much baseness of mind and violence of lust?

If it is said that such marriages are not absolutely unlawful, the rule of the civil law gives a good answer: “Always in marriages, not only what is lawful but what is honorable and fitting is to be considered.”

=== §. 11. #emph[Of equality in piety and religion between those who are to be married together.]

4\. The equality which is of greatest consequence between #footnote[4\. Equality in piety.] parties to be married is in #emph[piety.] In this respect it is needful that, as a Christian should be married to a Christian, and one who professes #footnote[#emph[E domibus Christianis coniugij quaeratur copula.] \[Let the bond of marriage be sought from Christian homes.\] #emph[Ambr. Epist.] 70.] the true faith to one who professes the same faith, so one who truly fears God should be married to one of the same mind and disposition, as is noted of Zechariah and Elizabeth:#footnote[Luke 1:6.] “Both of them were righteous in the sight of God,” etc. A worthy couple, each worthy of the other! Being both alike in such excellent qualities, they could not but reap much comfort and profit in every way from each other.

This is one of the chief points included in that proviso which the Holy Spirit gives for choosing a yokefellow, in these words: “in the Lord.” #footnote[1 Cor 7:39.]

There is no means like this equality to increase love, preserve peace, provoke to every duty, and make them helpful to one another in all things and at all times. By it they will be made both able to do more good to one another and capable of receiving more good from one another, especially in the best things, even in those which concern their spiritual edification in this world and their eternal salvation in the world to come. For Christ is the fountain and head of all spiritual life and grace (“God was pleased to have all His fullness #footnote[Col 1:19.] dwell in Him,” so that it is he “who fills all in all”). Now Christ imparts #footnote[Eph 1:23.] the life and grace which is in him to those who are members of his body. If then I, being a member of that mystical body, am linked by that close and inviolable bond of marriage to one of the members of that body, what great hope there is of our imparting to one another, and partaking from one another, those gifts and graces which each of us receives from Christ our head! If an unbelieving wife may be saved by a believing husband,#footnote[1 Cor 7:16.] and an unbelieving husband by a believing wife, much more will one believer be more and more edified by another.

Happy is that family where both its governors, husband and wife, are fellow members of Christ’s body. There the house will be made God’s church, as the house of Aquila and Priscilla was.#footnote[Rom 16:5.] God’s worship will be maintained there. Children will be trained up there in the nurture of the Lord, and servants also taught the fear of God. For those who are indeed of Christ’s kingdom will be like leaven, which #footnote[Matt 13:33.] seasons the whole lump. Take as an instance the declaration which Joshua #footnote[Josh 24:15.] makes to this purpose, and the effect which is noted of the ruler whose son Christ healed: “he and #footnote[John 4:53.] all his household believed.”

Here, by the way, let me exhort parents and other governors #footnote[Exhortation to marry in the Lord. See #emph[Treatise] 3, §. 68.] of children both to train up their own children in true piety and the fear of God, and also to seek such matches for them as they may have some assurance are of the same faith, and of the same mind and heart. So they will gain much happiness in marriage for their children, as Abraham did for Isaac. #footnote[Gen 24.]

If parents ought in this respect to seek the good of their children, much more ought those who are at their own disposal, and free to marry whom they will, to have special regard to equality in piety: especially women, because they are in subjection to their husbands. Yet men ought not to be careless in this either. For wives, if the fear of God does not possess their hearts, though they are the weaker vessels, often make their husbands their plain vassals.

=== §. 12. #emph[Of marriages between persons of different religious professions.]

Contrary are marriages with persons of different dispositions and #footnote[Deut 7:3. Neh 10:30.] different professions of religion, especially when they are made with unbelievers or idolaters.

This is one of those unequal yokes with which #footnote[2 Cor 6:14.] the apostle #footnote[Harms of unequal matches in religion.] forbids Christians to be yoked.

Great are the dangers and harms that ordinarily follow from it. For first, “the holy seed”#footnote[Ezra 9:2.] (to use the words of the Holy Spirit) is by this “mixed,” and so it comes about that “the holiness of the #smallcaps[Lord] is profaned,”#footnote[Mal 2:11.] that is, those whom God has chosen to be a holy nation and a people of his own are polluted.

#emph[Objection.] “The unbelieving husband is sanctified through his believing wife, and the unbelieving wife is sanctified through her believing husband.”#footnote[1 Cor 7:14.]

#emph[Answer.] That is noted only as a remedy for a trouble which, once it has come about, cannot be undone. When two unbelievers are married together, and one of them is converted to the true faith while the other remains an unbeliever, then, so that the believer might enjoy the company of the #footnote[#emph[Non de ijs qui adhuc coniuncti non sunt, sed de coniunctis agitur. Siquidem non dicit si quis vult accipere infidelem, sed si quis habet infidelem.] \[He is dealing not with those who are not yet joined, but with those who are joined. For he does not say, “If anyone wants to take an unbeliever,” but, “If anyone has an unbeliever.”\] #emph[Chrysost. in] 1 Cor 7. #emph[hom.] 19.] other with some comfort, the apostle shows that for the communion of marriage the unbeliever is sanctified by the believer. But by this he gives no liberty to believers to marry unbelievers. For though God in mercy sanctifies in this way the unequal yoke which cannot be broken, yet those who, professing God’s name, knowingly take on that yoke do as much as lies in them to profane God’s holiness.

2\. One who professes the true religion is in danger of apostasy by it. This reason #footnote[Deut 7:4.] the law itself presses against this point, and the Scripture gives many examples of its woeful outcome: as of Solomon,#footnote[1 Kgs 11:4.] the wisest among men; #footnote[Judg 16:16.] of Samson, the strongest; of Jehoram,#footnote[2 Kgs 8:18.] and others.

#emph[Objection.] If I marry one of a contrary religion, I may convert a sinner from going astray and save a soul from death.

#emph[Answer.] It is only #emph[a mere “may.”] You have no warrant to take that risk, but are forbidden it, to avoid the contrary danger. There is great cause to fear that in an unequal yoke the worse will prevail over the better. We are by nature prone to yield to error, falsehood and every kind of evil. Truth, virtue and grace go against the current of nature. It is likely to turn out in this unequal yoke as if two oxen in one yoke should pull opposite ways against each other on the side of a hill: the ox which pulls downhill, though it is a poor, lean, weak one, will pull down the other, though that is an able, vigorous, strong one. All evil is downhill; all goodness is uphill. And because of the flesh which remains in all as long as they remain on earth, those who have some goodness in them are prone and ready to fall away, but those who are evil are very obstinate in it. Yes, evil is also of a more spreading and infecting nature. A little sour leaven will soon make a great batch of dough sour, but a great deal of sweet dough will not sweeten a little leaven. It is therefore an unwarranted pretext for anyone to marry one of a contrary religion or disposition in the hope of converting them. It is not in man’s power to convert another. It is a divine work; without God’s blessing it cannot be done. There is little ground to expect a blessing from God in a course which is directly against his own ordinance. A curse may rather be feared. And the woeful experience of all ages has shown how God has cursed such marriages.

#emph[Objection.] Saint Peter#footnote[1 Pet 3:1.] exhorts wives so to conduct themselves that their husbands, who “do not obey the word,” may by that means “be won over without words.” And #footnote[1 Cor 7:16.] Saint Paul says to those who were unequally matched, “How do you know, wife, whether you will save your husband? Or how do you know, husband, whether you will save your wife?” It appears therefore that an evil husband may be converted by a good wife, and an evil wife by a good husband.

#emph[Answer.] These directions are given only as a remedy to redress such irreversible harms as have already happened, and not to give liberty to rush knowingly into them (as was noted before). When a misfortune has happened, necessity lies on a man, so that every means must be used to redress it. But to infer from this that a man may safely enter a course which draws him into those harms, what is that but to thrust himself into the fire, and to cast himself into temptation, #footnote[Matt 6:13.] which he is taught to pray against? Besides, the directions named above are not grounded on any express promises, but on mere suppositions.

3\. Many other harms are likely to follow from this kind of unequal marriage, besides the danger of apostasy (which is nevertheless the greatest), such as:

#[
#set enum(indent: 24pt)
+ #emph[Many quarrels and contentions between man and wife.]#footnote[#emph[Nihil grauius quam copulari alienigenae, vbi discordiae incentiua conflantur.] \[Nothing is more grievous than to be joined to a foreigner, where the kindlings of discord are fanned into flame.\] #emph[Ambr. Ep.] 70.] For how can there be mutual marriage concord between those who are of different minds?
+ #emph[Disturbance of the family:] one part siding with the man, another with the woman.
+ #emph[Wasting of goods:] the husband spending what he can on those who are of his profession of faith, and the wife what she can on those who are of hers.
+ #emph[Hindrance of all holy duties] in the house: the party who is of #footnote[#emph[Cum oratio communis esse debeat, quomodo inter dispares deuotione potest esse coniugij communis charitas?] \[Since prayer ought to be shared, how can there be the shared love of marriage between those who differ in devotion?\] #emph[Ibid.]] the contrary profession not enduring that there should be any.
+ #emph[Profaneness and atheism in children.] For commonly, when parents are of different religions, children are of none: #footnote[Lev 24:10, 11.] take as an instance that cursed blasphemer whose mother was an Israelite but his father an Egyptian.
]

The Scripture describes such children as #footnote[Gen 6:4.] monsters and #footnote[Neh 13:24.] mongrels.

#emph[Objection.] Salmon married Rahab,#footnote[Matt 1:5.] and Boaz married Ruth, both of which women were unbelievers.

#emph[Answer.] They had been so, but were not so when they married them. As faith and repentance alter the disposition, so they also alter the condition of the worst that can be. Rahab’s#footnote[Josh 2:9, etc. Heb 11:31.] faith was shown before she was married to Salmon; and Ruth#footnote[Ruth 1:16.] had forsaken her country’s gods and chosen the God of Israel to be her God, and Boaz#footnote[Ruth 2:11, 12.] knew as much.

#emph[Objection.] But no such thing can be said of the #footnote[Judg 14:1, etc.] daughter of the Philistines whom Samson married.

#emph[Answer.] This was an extraordinary matter, and therefore not an example for all. That he did it by an extraordinary impulse is evident from this phrase, “this was from the #smallcaps[Lord].” That it is not an example for others is evident, because #footnote[Deut 7:3.] God’s law, the rule of example, forbids it. God moved him to it to show his own #emph[power] and #emph[wisdom] by it.

His #emph[power,] in that he was able to work by contraries: to work enmity by friendship, utter destruction by close union. For God turned the heart of Samson, a chief man in Israel, to be drawn to and desire in marriage a daughter of the Philistines, and moved the parents of that maiden to give her to Samson in marriage: here was friendship and close union. Yet #footnote[Judg 15.] the history shows that by this means an occasion was given to Samson to avenge himself and the people of God on the Philistines.

His #emph[wisdom,] in that he turned what had an apparent show of great evil into much good. For the fact that Samson, a Nazirite of the Lord, a chief man in Israel, should join in marriage with a daughter of idolaters, who were also spiteful enemies of the Lord’s people, had a show of evil to those who did not know the spirit of Samson or the intent of the Lord. Yet by this means God brought Samson to be a judge and deliverer of his people.

It was by such a spirit as Elijah’s, when he called for fire from heaven,#footnote[2 Kgs 1:10.] that Samson married an idolater. If anyone shall think from this of marrying idolaters, I may say to them as Christ said to his disciples, “You#super[+] do not know what kind of spirit you#super[+] are of.”#footnote[Luke 9:55.] This example of Samson can be no more a warrant to others to marry idolaters than his practice of pulling down a house on his own head#footnote[Judg 16:30.] is a warrant to self-murderers. The warrant for extraordinary acts is particular, personal and belongs only to those who do them. But the warrant for acts meant as examples must be the general and known will of God in his word.

Whether this general rule may be set aside in some particular cases or not, I leave to the decision of more learned divines, and submit all to the judgment of God’s church.

=== §. 13. #emph[Of that mutual liking which must pass between marriageable persons before they are married.]

Having shown what #emph[persons] are fit to be joined in marriage, it remains to show in what #emph[manner] they are to be joined.

There are in Scripture three steps or degrees commended to us, by which marriageable parties are to proceed to marriage in order.

1\. A mutual liking.

2\. An actual contract.

3\. A public solemnizing of marriage.

I. The first liking is sometimes on the part of the parents or other relatives, and is then made known by them to the party to be married: as the family of Rebekah, liking the offer of Isaac #footnote[Gen 24:58.] which was made by Abraham’s servant, made it known to Rebekah herself. Sometimes again the first liking is on the part of the one who is to be married; and then, if that party is under the government of parents, the matter must be put to them before there is any further proceeding in it, as Samson, seeing and liking a daughter of the Philistines, #footnote[Judg 14:2.] told his father and his mother about it. Yes, even if the party is not under anyone’s government, yet it is very fitting that counsel be taken of wise and understanding friends, so that in a matter as weighty as marriage there may be the advice of more heads than one, to prevent such harms as might come about through rashness. After one party has in this way taken a liking to a fitting mate, that liking must be made known to the other party so liked, to learn whether there is a mutual affection of one toward the other. So Samson went and #footnote[Judg 14:7.] talked with the woman whom he liked to be his wife. If at first a good liking of one another is mutually and thoroughly settled in both their hearts, love is likely to continue in them forever, just as things which are well glued and set before they are shaken up and down will never come apart; but if they are joined together without glue, or shaken while the glue is wet, they cannot remain firm.

Mutual love and good liking of each other is like glue.

Let the parties to be married be well settled in this before they come to meet with trials through living together, and that love will not easily be loosened by any trials.

Contrary is the adulterous and brutish practice of those who, #footnote[Too eager and hasty pursuit of marriage, a brutish thing.] as soon as they cast their eye on anyone they like, never take advice or consult about a right and due proceeding to marriage, but at once, with all the eagerness and speed they can, like brute beasts, seek to have their desire and lust satisfied. Though, to keep themselves free from the penalty of the laws under which they live, they find means to be married, yet they show a lustful and adulterous mind. And their practice is too like the practice of the Benjamites,#footnote[Judg 21:23.] who seized wives from among the daughters of Shiloh as they were dancing, or else like the practice of the old world, which so grieved the Spirit of God#footnote[Gen 6:2.] that he regretted having made man, and so was moved to bring a flood over the whole world. Their practice was this, that “they took as wives whomever they chose”: that is, they married rashly and suddenly whomever they liked, without any consideration of their condition.
